I am testing open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object. The documented rule says: "The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text". I give two replies. Reply A is:
> ```javascript
> {"c":3}
> ```
> And here is another object:
> {"a":1}
Reply B is:
> ```json
> {"c":3}
> ```
> And here is another object:
> {"a":1}
The only difference is the fence label: javascript versus json. Under the rule, both replies have a code fence holding an object, and that fenced object is {"c":3}; the trailing object {"a":1} is not inside a fence. Therefore the rule requires the same answer for both: the object scored is {"c":3}. The function, however, as its code shows, only recognises fences labelled json, JSON, or unlabelled; for Reply A's javascript fence it finds no fenced candidate and falls back to the last top-level object, which is {"a":1}, while for Reply B it returns {"c":3}. So the function tells the two replies apart where the rule says it should not. This is the weaker kind of failure, not the original find of a pair the check answers the same on. I would be wrong if the function returns the same answer for both replies, for example if it returns {"c":3} for Reply A as well, because then it would not tell them apart.

